<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Shimashki dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">2100 BCE – 1970 BCE (approx.) · capital Susa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Elamite highland dynasty whose king Kindattu sacked Ur in 2004 BCE and carried off its last king, ending the Neo-Sumerian Empire.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shimashki was a group of highland lands in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, probably north of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Anshan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and Luristan. Under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> its rulers were both enemies and allies of Ur, receiving Sumerian princesses in marriage. As Ur weakened at the end of the 21st century BCE, Shimashki kings took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> king list names twelve Shimashki kings, but few are well documented. They ruled a federation of Elamite lands joined to Susa and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Anshan</a>, combining highland and lowland power, a pattern that later Elamite states kept. Religion centred on Elamite gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inshushinak">Inshushinak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humban">Humban</a>. The dynasty's greatest moment came when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kindattu">Kindattu</a> sacked Ur in 2004 BCE and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a> captive to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ishbi-erra">Ishbi-Erra</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/isin">Isin</a> expelled the Elamite garrison from Ur soon after. Around 1970 BCE power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sukkalmah-dynasty">Sukkalmah dynasty</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebarat-ii">Ebarat</a>, whose rulers continued to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shimashki-dynasty.jpg" alt="Shimashki dynasty" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Near_East_2000BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2100 BCE – 1970 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Susa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kindattu, Idaddu I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Elamite religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elamite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shimashki, Simashki</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2035&amp;focus=shimashki-dynasty" aria-label="Shimashki dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5921 1007 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shimashki dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6241 1263L6241 1263M6241 1263L6193 1305M6241 1263L6241 1263"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6241 1263h0M6193 1305h0M6241 1263h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1263h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1263h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Susa. <a href="/atlas?year=-2035&amp;focus=shimashki-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
